WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.480
Impregnate and test.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.636
Lecture five of the Manufacturing module.

3
00:00:06.552 --> 00:00:13.560
Lecture four left our EV-size stator, 48 slots and 8 poles, welded and coated:

4
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.736
102 joints.

5
00:00:15.736 --> 00:00:19.812
Now it is sealed in resin, and then it must prove it works.

6
00:00:20.568 --> 00:00:21.912
The steps:

7
00:00:21.912 --> 00:00:25.464
heat, trickle, gel and cure.

8
00:00:25.464 --> 00:00:26.776
Then the tests:

9
00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:34.296
resistance, insulation resistance, the high-voltage test called hipot, surge and partial discharge.

10
00:00:34.296 --> 00:00:36.772
And a decision on any stator that fails.

11
00:00:37.784 --> 00:00:44.194
The resistances, voltages and resin volumes here come from our Hairpin Motor Designer, run

12
00:00:44.194 --> 00:00:45.188
on that stator.

13
00:00:46.388 --> 00:00:47.412
Chapter one.

14
00:00:47.412 --> 00:00:48.448
Why resin.

15
00:00:49.744 --> 00:00:51.600
Look inside one slot.

16
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.928
Our 3.6-millimetre bars leave 0.15 millimetres on each side, between bar and liner.

17
00:00:58.928 --> 00:01:02.940
With the gaps between the layers, 12 percent of the slot is still air.

18
00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:06.928
Resin fills that air, for three reasons.

19
00:01:06.928 --> 00:01:11.613
First, it glues the pins to each other and to the liner, so vibration

20
00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:14.364
does not rub the enamel or work on the welds.

21
00:01:14.928 --> 00:01:16.816
Second, heat.

22
00:01:16.816 --> 00:01:19.728
The copper's heat must cross into the steel.

23
00:01:19.728 --> 00:01:24.048
Air conducts about 0.026 watts per metre-kelvin;

24
00:01:24.048 --> 00:01:28.252
an impregnating resin about 0.21, eight times more.

25
00:01:29.296 --> 00:01:34.320
Between copper and steel lie the enamel, that gap and the liner.

26
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.152
In our slot the air gap holds two thirds of their thermal resistance.

27
00:01:39.152 --> 00:01:43.440
Fill it with resin and the three resist less than half as much:

28
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.592
at 10 amps per square millimetre, the temperature drop falls from 33 kelvin to

29
00:01:48.592 --> 00:01:51.228
13, in our one-dimensional estimate.

30
00:01:51.984 --> 00:01:53.872
Third, protection:

31
00:01:53.872 --> 00:01:59.180
resin keeps out moisture and oil, and removes the air where small sparks, partial

32
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.476
discharges, can start.

33
00:02:01.716 --> 00:02:02.932
Chapter two.

34
00:02:02.932 --> 00:02:04.000
Trickle and cure.

35
00:02:05.776 --> 00:02:12.080
The resins are polyesterimides, polyesters or epoxies that harden with heat.

36
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.688
Viscosity, the resistance to flow, decides the process:

37
00:02:16.688 --> 00:02:22.335
one resin sold for EV motors is like honey at room temperature, about 2,200

38
00:02:22.335 --> 00:02:27.856
millipascal-seconds, and like oil at 80 degrees, about 100.

39
00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.788
So the stator is heated first.

40
00:02:30.768 --> 00:02:34.428
Dipping the stator in a tank wastes resin and leaves it where it is

41
00:02:34.428 --> 00:02:36.080
not wanted;

42
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:41.296
vacuum-pressure impregnation, VPI, suits large machines.

43
00:02:41.296 --> 00:02:44.956
For car motors, one study calls trickling the usual method.

44
00:02:46.096 --> 00:02:47.312
Trickling:

45
00:02:47.312 --> 00:02:52.406
the hot stator turns slowly on a shaft, tilted a few degrees, while nozzles

46
00:02:52.406 --> 00:02:54.588
drip resin onto both end windings.

47
00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.160
Capillary action, the pull that draws liquid into a thin gap, carries it into

48
00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.776
the slots.

49
00:03:01.776 --> 00:03:08.144
On an eight-layer, 48-slot stator, one study timed 35 minutes to the far end;

50
00:03:08.144 --> 00:03:12.316
simulated without capillary forces, no resin arrived in 45.

51
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.113
In our slot, resin fills the side gaps, the gaps between layers and the

52
00:03:18.113 --> 00:03:19.024
corners:

53
00:03:19.024 --> 00:03:21.264
11 square millimetres.

54
00:03:21.264 --> 00:03:28.686
Over the 120-millimetre stack and 48 slots, that is 64 cubic centimetres, about 75

55
00:03:28.686 --> 00:03:30.128
grams.

56
00:03:30.128 --> 00:03:33.180
A film on the end windings could add half as much again.

57
00:03:34.224 --> 00:03:39.504
Only as much is dripped as the winding takes, so the bore stays clean.

58
00:03:39.504 --> 00:03:42.012
The stator is weighed before and after.

59
00:03:42.832 --> 00:03:47.523
The resin gels, thickening to a jelly, while the stator keeps turning so none

60
00:03:47.523 --> 00:03:49.840
runs out, then cures:

61
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.768
for that resin, 15 to 30 minutes at 130 degrees.

62
00:03:54.768 --> 00:03:57.052
Some lines coat the weld heads only now.

63
00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.135
Chapter three.

64
00:04:03.135 --> 00:04:03.935
Resistance.

65
00:04:05.403 --> 00:04:09.946
A four-wire meter drives about one amp through two of the stator's leads and

66
00:04:09.946 --> 00:04:14.822
measures the voltage with a second pair of probes, so contact resistance does not

67
00:04:14.822 --> 00:04:15.622
count.

68
00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:21.115
Resistance is rho, the resistivity, times length over area.

69
00:04:21.115 --> 00:04:25.725
For copper at 20 degrees, rho is one fifty-eighth of an ohm for one

70
00:04:25.725 --> 00:04:28.475
metre of one square millimetre.

71
00:04:28.475 --> 00:04:34.587
One path of our winding is 7.49 metres of 14.15 square millimetres:

72
00:04:34.587 --> 00:04:37.019
9.12 milliohms.

73
00:04:37.019 --> 00:04:39.911
Its 17 joints bring it to 9.17.

74
00:04:40.699 --> 00:04:43.483
Each phase has two paths in parallel:

75
00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.915
4.59 milliohms.

76
00:04:45.915 --> 00:04:50.189
The star point is inside the stator, so the meter sees two phases in

77
00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.195
series.

78
00:04:51.195 --> 00:04:52.923
Between U and V:

79
00:04:52.923 --> 00:04:55.079
9.17 milliohms again.

80
00:04:56.315 --> 00:05:00.347
Copper's resistance rises about 0.4 percent per kelvin:

81
00:05:00.347 --> 00:05:05.115
at 150 degrees our phase is 6.93 milliohms.

82
00:05:05.115 --> 00:05:09.671
So the tester measures the temperature and corrects every reading to 20 degrees.

83
00:05:10.619 --> 00:05:15.683
The three readings, U to V, V to W and W to U, must

84
00:05:15.683 --> 00:05:20.635
agree with each other and the design, within a window the maker sets.

85
00:05:20.635 --> 00:05:23.067
A missing weld opens one path:

86
00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:26.311
that phase doubles, and two readings jump by half.

87
00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:29.595
A poor weld is another story.

88
00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:34.907
In lecture four, a gapped joint measured five micro-ohms more than a good one.

89
00:05:34.907 --> 00:05:39.373
Shared with the parallel path, that lifts the U to V reading by 1.2

90
00:05:39.373 --> 00:05:45.887
micro-ohms, 0.013 percent, as much as a temperature error of four hundredths of a

91
00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:47.099
kelvin.

92
00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:48.859
The meter could resolve it;

93
00:05:48.859 --> 00:05:50.779
no temperature correction can.

94
00:05:50.779 --> 00:05:53.223
So every weld was checked as it was made.

95
00:05:58.388 --> 00:05:59.476
Chapter four.

96
00:05:59.476 --> 00:06:01.248
Insulation under voltage.

97
00:06:02.576 --> 00:06:04.560
Three boundaries must hold:

98
00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.932
phase to core, phase to phase, and turn to turn.

99
00:06:08.944 --> 00:06:12.752
First, what an 800-volt motor sees.

100
00:06:12.752 --> 00:06:17.296
The 800 volts is the battery's DC voltage, the DC bus.

101
00:06:17.296 --> 00:06:20.176
The inverter turns it into AC:

102
00:06:20.176 --> 00:06:26.631
between two leads, the line voltage reaches at most about 566 volts r.m.s., and

103
00:06:26.631 --> 00:06:28.700
its peaks touch the full 800.

104
00:06:29.616 --> 00:06:34.049
Our model knows each conductor's place in its path, so it maps the voltage

105
00:06:34.049 --> 00:06:37.980
between neighbours at that full voltage, without switching spikes.

106
00:06:38.864 --> 00:06:40.368
Here is the surprise:

107
00:06:40.368 --> 00:06:45.701
our crown spans five slots, not six, so the even layers sit one slot

108
00:06:45.701 --> 00:06:46.960
over.

109
00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:53.360
Half the neighbouring pairs in the slots, 72 of 144, are two different phases,

110
00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.068
with only enamel between them.

111
00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:01.105
The worst pair, the first conductors after the leads, sees 97 percent of the

112
00:07:01.105 --> 00:07:02.448
line peak:

113
00:07:02.448 --> 00:07:07.472
about 780 volts on an 800-volt bus, before spikes.

114
00:07:07.472 --> 00:07:11.036
With a six-slot crown, no slot would hold two phases.

115
00:07:11.696 --> 00:07:13.168
Is that unusual?

116
00:07:13.168 --> 00:07:14.096
No.

117
00:07:14.096 --> 00:07:19.536
Every two-layer winding with a shortened crown has slots shared by two phases.

118
00:07:19.536 --> 00:07:24.637
Makers keep the short pitch because it cuts harmonics, and arrange the connections so

119
00:07:24.637 --> 00:07:27.504
the conductors next to the leads never meet;

120
00:07:27.504 --> 00:07:30.192
patents describe exactly that.

121
00:07:30.192 --> 00:07:34.928
In our stator only 3 of the 72 pairs exceed 90 percent of the

122
00:07:34.928 --> 00:07:37.532
line peak, and the average is about half.

123
00:07:38.672 --> 00:07:39.696
Test one:

124
00:07:39.696 --> 00:07:41.712
insulation resistance.

125
00:07:41.712 --> 00:07:46.608
500 or 1,000 volts DC go between winding and core;

126
00:07:46.608 --> 00:07:51.683
one standard asks at least five megohms, and one maker of test gear sees

127
00:07:51.683 --> 00:07:53.948
up to ten gigaohms on new parts.

128
00:07:54.992 --> 00:07:56.016
Test two:

129
00:07:56.016 --> 00:08:00.496
the hipot, short for high potential, the withstand test.

130
00:08:00.496 --> 00:08:04.903
The three leads are joined, the core is grounded, and an AC voltage far

131
00:08:04.903 --> 00:08:10.385
above the working voltage is applied, so weak insulation fails here, not in the

132
00:08:10.385 --> 00:08:11.568
car.

133
00:08:11.568 --> 00:08:17.168
The machine standard sets twice the rated voltage plus 1,000 volts, for a minute,

134
00:08:17.168 --> 00:08:22.064
or, in series production, 120 percent for one second.

135
00:08:22.064 --> 00:08:23.164
Nothing may break down.

136
00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.584
The standard's U is the motor's AC line voltage, so for an 800-volt battery

137
00:08:29.584 --> 00:08:32.272
U is 566 volts:

138
00:08:32.272 --> 00:08:36.976
2.1 kilovolts for a minute, or 2.6 for a second.

139
00:08:36.976 --> 00:08:43.344
Taking the full 800 instead, a stricter reading, gives 2.6 and 3.1.

140
00:08:43.344 --> 00:08:49.277
Our neutral ring joins the phases, so this test, like insulation resistance, checks winding

141
00:08:49.277 --> 00:08:51.292
to core, not phase to phase.

142
00:08:52.272 --> 00:08:53.264
Test three:

143
00:08:53.264 --> 00:08:54.064
surge.

144
00:08:54.064 --> 00:08:59.376
A capacitor discharges into two phases and the winding rings like a bell;

145
00:08:59.376 --> 00:09:01.936
all three pairs should ring alike.

146
00:09:01.936 --> 00:09:05.148
A shorted turn rings faster and dies sooner.

147
00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.944
But our path has only 16 turns.

148
00:09:08.944 --> 00:09:15.792
A common surge level, twice the bus voltage plus 1,000 volts, is 2.6 kilovolts.

149
00:09:15.792 --> 00:09:21.795
Shared evenly over two phases, each turn gets about 80 volts, below the roughly

150
00:09:21.795 --> 00:09:25.008
350 any air gap needs to spark.

151
00:09:25.008 --> 00:09:30.219
So surge finds shorts and wrong connections, not weak enamel between one turn and

152
00:09:30.219 --> 00:09:31.019
the next.

153
00:09:31.312 --> 00:09:32.624
Test four:

154
00:09:32.624 --> 00:09:38.159
partial discharge, small sparks in air gaps that do not bridge the insulation but

155
00:09:38.159 --> 00:09:40.656
slowly eat the enamel.

156
00:09:40.656 --> 00:09:45.205
The tester raises a voltage between leads, or to the core, and listens for

157
00:09:45.205 --> 00:09:46.736
the first discharges:

158
00:09:46.736 --> 00:09:48.028
the inception voltage.

159
00:09:48.816 --> 00:09:53.008
An 800-volt traction motor is designed to stay free of them.

160
00:09:53.008 --> 00:09:59.376
One European project set the limit between phases at 1,920 volts peak to peak:

161
00:09:59.376 --> 00:10:03.344
twice the bus voltage, plus 20 percent for spikes.

162
00:10:03.344 --> 00:10:08.304
Our worst slot pair swings 1,550 before spikes.

163
00:10:08.304 --> 00:10:15.568
That project found 110-micrometre enamel enough at 180 degrees, and 80 not.

164
00:10:15.568 --> 00:10:17.180
Ours is 120.

165
00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.684
All these run before the resin, when some faults can still be fixed, and

166
00:10:22.684 --> 00:10:23.516
again after.

167
00:10:28.607 --> 00:10:29.759
Chapter five.

168
00:10:29.759 --> 00:10:30.987
Find and reject.

169
00:10:32.475 --> 00:10:34.683
Each test has its faults.

170
00:10:34.683 --> 00:10:37.083
Resistance finds open paths;

171
00:10:37.083 --> 00:10:41.659
insulation resistance and hipot, weak insulation to the core;

172
00:10:41.659 --> 00:10:45.403
surge, shorted turns and wrong connections;

173
00:10:45.403 --> 00:10:49.159
partial discharge, thin enamel or bars too close.

174
00:10:49.883 --> 00:10:51.739
Did the resin get in?

175
00:10:51.739 --> 00:10:53.691
The scale says how much.

176
00:10:53.691 --> 00:10:56.251
Our model suggests a second clue:

177
00:10:56.251 --> 00:11:01.398
if the gaps fill, the capacitance to the core rises by half, from 6.9

178
00:11:01.398 --> 00:11:03.751
to 10.6 nanofarads.

179
00:11:04.827 --> 00:11:08.923
A bad weld caught before the heads are coated can be repaired.

180
00:11:08.923 --> 00:11:13.659
One patent cuts it out and joins the ends by arc welding with filler,

181
00:11:13.659 --> 00:11:17.511
as a second laser weld on the shortened pins would overheat the enamel.

182
00:11:18.395 --> 00:11:23.536
But once twisted, no pin can be changed economically, and we found no published

183
00:11:23.536 --> 00:11:25.787
repair for a cured winding:

184
00:11:25.787 --> 00:11:30.791
such a stator is most likely scrap, with its 5.8 kilograms of copper.

185
00:11:31.899 --> 00:11:34.363
The arithmetic is unforgiving.

186
00:11:34.363 --> 00:11:38.512
If one weld in a thousand is bad, almost one in ten of our

187
00:11:38.512 --> 00:11:40.731
stators carries one;

188
00:11:40.731 --> 00:11:43.931
with 200 welds, nearly one in five.

189
00:11:43.931 --> 00:11:50.427
For 99 good stators in 100, fewer than one weld in 10,000 may fail.

190
00:11:50.427 --> 00:11:53.543
So every result is stored under the stator's code.

191
00:11:54.645 --> 00:11:55.445
Summary.

192
00:11:55.413 --> 00:11:56.321
Four points.

193
00:11:57.489 --> 00:11:58.353
One.

194
00:11:58.353 --> 00:12:00.657
Resin fills the gaps of the winding:

195
00:12:00.657 --> 00:12:05.097
it fixes the pins, in our estimate more than halves the heat resistance to

196
00:12:05.097 --> 00:12:07.133
the steel, and keeps out moisture.

197
00:12:07.825 --> 00:12:08.625
Two.

198
00:12:08.593 --> 00:12:11.377
The stator is trickled hot and turning;

199
00:12:11.377 --> 00:12:15.229
capillary action fills the gaps, then the resin gels and cures.

200
00:12:15.953 --> 00:12:16.849
Three.

201
00:12:16.849 --> 00:12:21.681
Resistance shows the paths are complete, not that every weld is good:

202
00:12:21.681 --> 00:12:25.725
one poor weld moves our reading by 0.013 percent.

203
00:12:26.513 --> 00:12:27.377
Four.

204
00:12:27.377 --> 00:12:34.405
Insulation resistance, hipot, surge and partial discharge check the insulation, before and after the

205
00:12:34.405 --> 00:12:35.409
resin.

206
00:12:35.409 --> 00:12:39.293
With our five-slot crown, half the slot neighbours are two phases.

207
00:12:40.209 --> 00:12:42.321
That is the end of lecture five.

208
00:12:42.321 --> 00:12:44.017
Next, lecture six:

209
00:12:44.017 --> 00:12:48.209
into the motor, where the stator meets its housing and rotor.

210
00:12:48.209 --> 00:12:51.325
Until then, explore the finished stator in the free Viewer.

211
00:12:52.145 --> 00:12:57.713
Motoreka is a product of UMPSA, developed at EMDLab.

212
00:12:57.713 --> 00:12:59.197
motoreka.my.

